WEBVTT

1
00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:16.111
After Mr.

2
00:00:16.151 --> 00:00:23.297
Wilson left Holmes' study at 221B Baker Street, my friend sat in his favorite chair by the fireplace,

3
00:00:23.577 --> 00:00:27.240
smoked his pipe, and pondered the mystery of the Red-Headed League.

4
00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:34.286
Suddenly Holmes burst from the chair like a jack-in-the-box, placed his pipe down upon the table, and, turning

5
00:00:34.326 --> 00:00:38.009
to me, said, "There is a concert this afternoon at St.

6
00:00:38.069 --> 00:00:38.909
James' Hall.

7
00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:41.712
Could your patients spare you for a few hours?"

8
00:00:43.153 --> 00:00:45.795
"I have no more appointments today," I answered.

9
00:00:45.815 --> 00:00:48.117
"Then put on your hat and come.

10
00:00:48.958 --> 00:00:51.160
We'll go through the city and have lunch on the way.

11
00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:53.401
The program looks excellent."

12
00:00:54.422 --> 00:00:58.417
I have known Holmes for years, so I knew not to question his plan.

13
00:00:59.298 --> 00:01:05.022
What may seem strange or trivial to me always turns out to be appropriate and a significant part

14
00:01:05.062 --> 00:01:05.642
of the case.

15
00:01:06.763 --> 00:01:08.464
We traveled by the Underground.

16
00:01:08.884 --> 00:01:12.326
When we got off, we walked a short distance to Coburg Square.

17
00:01:13.227 --> 00:01:14.568
It was a shabby neighborhood.

18
00:01:15.248 --> 00:01:19.070
On one of the buildings there was a sign: Wilson's Pawnshop.

19
00:01:19.771 --> 00:01:21.372
Holmes and I approached the building.

20
00:01:22.124 --> 00:01:27.368
First, Holmes studied it carefully; then, he walked up and down the block and studied each of the

21
00:01:27.408 --> 00:01:28.890
other buildings in the neighborhood.

22
00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:32.152
When he was satisfied, he returned to Mr.

23
00:01:32.212 --> 00:01:32.993
Wilson's building.

24
00:01:33.733 --> 00:01:38.157
He struck the pavement two or three times with his walking stick, and then he went up to

25
00:01:38.197 --> 00:01:39.158
the door and knocked.

26
00:01:40.098 --> 00:01:44.722
The door was opened by a bright, clean-shaven fellow, who said, "Step in, please."

27
00:01:44.742 --> 00:01:49.426
"Thank you," said Holmes, "but I'm in need of directions.

28
00:01:49.826 --> 00:01:51.788
How can I get to the Strand Theater?"

29
00:01:52.428 --> 00:01:58.213
"Take that street over there, then take the third right, and then the fourth left," answered the assistant

30
00:01:58.313 --> 00:01:59.474
and promptly closed the door.

31
00:01:59.494 --> 00:02:03.637
"Smart fellow," said Holmes as we walked away.

32
00:02:03.657 --> 00:02:09.942
"In my opinion he is the second smartest man in London, and if I end up being wrong

33
00:02:09.982 --> 00:02:13.505
about him, he will have every right to be called the first."

34
00:02:14.546 --> 00:02:15.066
"You know him?"

35
00:02:15.667 --> 00:02:16.027
I asked.

36
00:02:16.047 --> 00:02:22.152
"I know of him, but this is the first time our paths have crossed," answered Holmes.

37
00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:28.604
"Mr. Wilson's assistant certainly plays a major role in this mystery, so you asked for directions to the

38
00:02:28.644 --> 00:02:31.006
Strand in order to look at him," I said.

39
00:02:31.026 --> 00:02:33.827
"Not him," said Holmes.

40
00:02:34.448 --> 00:02:34.888
"What then?"

41
00:02:35.328 --> 00:02:36.289
I said, surprised.

42
00:02:36.309 --> 00:02:40.032
"The knees of his pants," answered Holmes.

43
00:02:40.792 --> 00:02:41.533
"What did you see?"

44
00:02:42.153 --> 00:02:42.713
I inquired.

45
00:02:42.733 --> 00:02:46.816
"What I expected," said Holmes mysteriously.

46
00:02:46.836 --> 00:02:49.078
"Why did you beat the pavement?"

47
00:02:49.598 --> 00:02:49.998
I asked.

48
00:02:50.018 --> 00:02:58.845
"My dear doctor," replied Holmes almost inaudibly, "this is a time of observation and reflection, not talk."

49
00:02:59.745 --> 00:03:05.389
When we got to the Strand Theater, Holmes stopped and said, "Sorry to have been so abrupt, but

50
00:03:05.449 --> 00:03:07.791
we were spies in the enemy's territory.

51
00:03:08.432 --> 00:03:10.913
It was risky to talk about the case back there.

52
00:03:11.574 --> 00:03:15.997
Now that we know something of Coburg Square, let us explore more of this neighborhood."

53
00:03:16.697 --> 00:03:21.953
The Strand Theater is on one of the main roads of the city, a diagonal road that allows

54
00:03:22.033 --> 00:03:24.454
traffic to flow north west across the city.

55
00:03:25.254 --> 00:03:28.796
We followed this main street back in the direction of Coburg Square.

56
00:03:28.816 --> 00:03:33.698
"Let me see, we've passed through three intersections," said Holmes.

57
00:03:34.458 --> 00:03:36.039
"Yes, this is about right."

58
00:03:36.679 --> 00:03:42.261
It was difficult to believe that this busy street is so close to the faded, stagnant, and forgotten

59
00:03:42.341 --> 00:03:43.182
Coburg Square.

60
00:03:43.202 --> 00:03:48.264
"I'd like to remember these buildings exactly," said Holmes to himself.

61
00:03:48.884 --> 00:03:55.306
"There is the tobacco shop, the little newspaper shop, the Coburg branch of the City and Suburban Bank,

62
00:03:55.786 --> 00:03:59.227
a vegetarian restaurant, and McFarlane's department store."

63
00:04:00.207 --> 00:04:04.688
I tried to see London as I knew my friend was seeing London, but I knew I was

64
00:04:04.748 --> 00:04:06.249
missing subtle details.

65
00:04:06.269 --> 00:04:12.871
"And now, Doctor," said Holmes, "our work is done, so it's time to have some fun.

66
00:04:13.591 --> 00:04:19.213
A sandwich and a cup of coffee, and then off to violin-land, where all is sweetness and harmony,

67
00:04:19.653 --> 00:04:23.855
and where there are no red-headed clients to trouble us with their problems."

68
00:04:24.715 --> 00:04:27.156
My friend is an enthusiastic music lover.

69
00:04:27.876 --> 00:04:32.638
He enjoys playing the violin almost as much as he enjoys listening to others play.

70
00:04:33.399 --> 00:04:34.779
We sat together in St.

71
00:04:34.839 --> 00:04:38.661
James' Hall, far removed from the mystery of the Red-Headed League.

72
00:04:39.501 --> 00:04:45.683
Although I enjoyed the music, I couldn't stop myself from thinking that something quite unpleasant awaited the men

73
00:04:45.744 --> 00:04:46.684
Holmes was seeking.

74
00:04:47.604 --> 00:04:51.866
My friend is a ruthless competitor and he takes the game very seriously.

75
00:04:52.887 --> 00:04:58.170
After the concert, Holmes turned to me and said, "You, no doubt, want to go home, Doctor."

76
00:04:58.190 --> 00:05:02.992
"Yes," I replied, "I would like to freshen up and have a pot of tea."

77
00:05:03.773 --> 00:05:05.514
"Very well," said Holmes.

78
00:05:05.534 --> 00:05:10.376
"I still have some business to attend to, which will take several hours.

79
00:05:11.177 --> 00:05:14.038
This affair at Coburg Square is quite critical."

80
00:05:14.058 --> 00:05:15.539
"Why is that?"

81
00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:16.540
I asked.

82
00:05:17.416 --> 00:05:25.924
"I believe a serious crime has been planned," replied Holmes, "but I have every reason to believe that

83
00:05:25.984 --> 00:05:27.885
we will be in time to stop it.

84
00:05:28.786 --> 00:05:32.469
Today being Saturday makes things a little more complicated.

85
00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:34.911
I'll want your help tonight, Doctor."

86
00:05:34.931 --> 00:05:36.052
"What time?"

87
00:05:36.633 --> 00:05:37.013
I asked.

88
00:05:37.033 --> 00:05:41.236
"I think ten this evening will be fine," said Holmes.

89
00:05:41.256 --> 00:05:45.180
"I will meet you at Baker Street at ten," I replied.

90
00:05:46.188 --> 00:05:51.653
I was in the process of hailing a carriage, when Holmes suddenly turned to me and said, "Doctor,

91
00:05:51.693 --> 00:05:56.517
there may be some risk tonight, so please put your army revolver in your pocket."

92
00:05:57.698 --> 00:06:02.743
With a smile he turned, waved his hand, and disappeared almost instantly into the crowd.

93
00:06:03.684 --> 00:06:08.908
If Holmes thought there might be danger, I knew that there was good reason for it, but I

94
00:06:08.968 --> 00:06:12.892
felt frustrated at not understanding why we might be in jeopardy.

95
00:06:14.152 --> 00:06:18.773
The carriage ride home was joyless because I felt more stupid than usual.

96
00:06:20.054 --> 00:06:24.055
I often suffer from these feelings when I am around Holmes.

97
00:06:24.075 --> 00:06:29.577
I heard what he heard, I saw what he saw, and I imagine that I thought the way

98
00:06:29.657 --> 00:06:36.639
he had thought; however, whereas I had found no solution to our mystery, my friend not only understood

99
00:06:36.679 --> 00:06:40.480
what had happened, but also seemed to know what was about to happen.

100
00:06:41.572 --> 00:06:47.534
I reviewed everything I knew about the Red-Headed League and our visit to Coburg Square, but found it

101
00:06:47.594 --> 00:06:53.916
futile. The exercise produced more questions than answers: Where were we going tonight?

102
00:06:54.596 --> 00:06:56.656
What kind of crime was going to be committed?

103
00:06:57.437 --> 00:06:58.577
How would we stop it?

104
00:06:59.357 --> 00:07:01.278
Why was a revolver necessary?

105
00:07:02.158 --> 00:07:04.799
The only question I thought I could answer was who.

106
00:07:06.039 --> 00:07:12.968
I knew that the assistant was somehow involved, but my curiosity wouldn't be satisfied until that night.

107
00:07:14.349 --> 00:07:17.752
I spent a restless evening at home and then started for Baker Street.

108
00:07:18.633 --> 00:07:21.936
When I arrived, I saw Holmes standing on the street with two men.

109
00:07:21.956 --> 00:07:26.341
"Ah, Watson," he greeted me, looking at his watch.

110
00:07:26.361 --> 00:07:28.663
"Perfectly punctual, as usual.

111
00:07:29.203 --> 00:07:31.045
I think you are acquainted with Mr.

112
00:07:31.085 --> 00:07:35.209
Jones, the inspector from Scotland Yard, and this is Mr.

113
00:07:35.229 --> 00:07:39.614
Merryweather, the vice president of the Coburg branch of the City and Suburban Bank.

114
00:07:40.655 --> 00:07:43.438
They will be our companions in tonight's undertaking."

115
00:07:44.439 --> 00:07:50.024
I nodded to both men and was surprised to find myself rather excited and impatient to embark on

116
00:07:50.064 --> 00:07:51.426
this perilous adventure.
